# Acknowledged package-lock.json decisions.
#
# `npm run check:lockfile` gates on three things. Two of them are questions
# rather than defects — a direct dependency moving backwards, and a change to
# `overrides` — and its output asks "Intentional?" with nowhere to answer.
# This file is the answer, written in the SAME commit as the change it
# describes so it is reviewed as part of that diff.
#
# Format — one decision per line, reason after the `#`:
#
#   downgrade <lockfile-key> <from> -> <to>   # why, with an issue number
#   overrides <key> <from> -> <to>            # why, with an issue number
#
# Both name a TRANSITION. For `overrides`, use the literal `none` for a side
# where the entry does not exist, and one line per key — so removing an override
# is a separate decision from adding it. That matters: dropping an override that
# was fixing a CVE walks the patched transitive back to a vulnerable version,
# and an acknowledgement keyed on the resulting block would wave it through
# because the block returns to an earlier, already-approved shape.
#
# When the check gates it prints the exact line to add. Copy it verbatim: the
# package field is the lockfile's own path key (`node_modules/@types/node`, and
# `node_modules/a/node_modules/b` for a nested copy), not the bare package name.
#
# Entries are keyed to EXACT versions, so one acknowledges precisely one move.
# A later, DIFFERENT downgrade of the same package is a new decision and fires
# the gate again, which is what stops an entry becoming a blanket exemption.
#
# Be aware of the limit: an entry acknowledges its exact transition FOREVER, so
# a diff that repeats the identical (package, from, to) move — a revert of a
# revert, or a fork sync that replays it — is waved through silently. That is
# the price of entries being safe to leave in place, which they have to be:
# once a PR merges its entry no longer matches anything, and four live forks
# inherit this whole log and would otherwise fail every sync. Treat the file as
# a decision log rather than something to prune.
#
# NOT acknowledgeable: lost `libc` / `os` / `cpu`. Never a decision — npm below
# 11.11.0 drops the key on every platform, the lockfile then installs fine
# locally and wrong on Alpine (#571), and there is a repair:
# `npm run fix:lockfile-libc`.
#
# See scripts/ci/lockfile-decisions.ts and .context/architecture/ci.md.

downgrade node_modules/@types/node 26.2.0 -> 24.13.3   # pinned to the runtime major — tsc was type-checking against a stdlib two majors ahead of every runtime (#584)
